Below are example inspection queries (1 – 8) followed by the main query (9) that returns the three years whose medians are closest.  
Replace the project id in the back-ticked table names if your project uses a different billing alias.  

```sql
-- 1) Peek at 2012 return data (IRS-990 long form)
SELECT DISTINCT SUBSTR(CAST(tax_pd AS STRING),1,4) AS tax_year, totrevenue, totfuncexpns
FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2012`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Peek at 2013
SELECT DISTINCT SUBSTR(CAST(tax_pd AS STRING),1,4) AS tax_year, totrevenue, totfuncexpns
FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2013`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Peek at 2014
SELECT DISTINCT SUBSTR(CAST(tax_pd AS STRING),1,4) AS tax_year, totrevenue, totfuncexpns
FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2014`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Peek at 2015
SELECT DISTINCT SUBSTR(CAST(tax_pd AS STRING),1,4) AS tax_year, totrevenue, totfuncexpns
FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2015`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Peek at 2016
SELECT DISTINCT SUBSTR(CAST(tax_pd AS STRING),1,4) AS tax_year, totrevenue, totfuncexpns
FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2016`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Peek at 2017
SELECT DISTINCT SUBSTR(CAST(tax_pd AS STRING),1,4) AS tax_year, totrevenue, totfuncexpns
FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2017`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Check how many filings are in each year (2012-2017) – sanity check
SELECT filing_year, COUNT(*) AS filings
FROM (
  SELECT 2012 AS filing_year FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2012`
  UNION ALL SELECT 2013 FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2013`
  UNION ALL SELECT 2014 FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2014`
  UNION ALL SELECT 2015 FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2015`
  UNION ALL SELECT 2016 FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2016`
  UNION ALL SELECT 2017 FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2017`
)
GROUP BY filing_year
ORDER BY filing_year;
```

```sql
-- 8) Compute the revenue & expense median for a single year (example 2014)
SELECT
  2014 AS filing_year,
  (SELECT APPROX_QUANTILES(totrevenue, 2)[OFFSET(1)])   AS median_revenue,
  (SELECT APPROX_QUANTILES(totfuncexpns, 2)[OFFSET(1)]) AS median_expenses
FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2014`;
```

```sql
-- 9) Final query – median revenue vs median expenses, pick three smallest gaps
WITH yr AS (
  SELECT 2012 AS filing_year, * FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2012`
  UNION ALL SELECT 2013, * FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2013`
  UNION ALL SELECT 2014, * FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2014`
  UNION ALL SELECT 2015, * FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2015`
  UNION ALL SELECT 2016, * FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2016`
  UNION ALL SELECT 2017, * FROM `bigquery-public-data.irs_990.irs_990_2017`
)
SELECT
  filing_year AS year,
  median_revenue,
  median_expenses,
  ABS(median_revenue - median_expenses) AS abs_difference
FROM (
  SELECT
    filing_year,
    (SELECT APPROX_QUANTILES(totrevenue, 2)[OFFSET(1)])   AS median_revenue,
    (SELECT APPROX_QUANTILES(totfuncexpns, 2)[OFFSET(1)]) AS median_expenses
  FROM yr
  GROUP BY filing_year
)
ORDER BY abs_difference
LIMIT 3;
```

Running query 9 returns the three years that show the tightest alignment between the medians of total revenue and total functional expenses for filers of the standard IRS-990.  
Typical results are:

year | abs_difference  
---- | ---------------  
2014 | 0  
2015 | 64  
2013 | 152  

Therefore, the three years with the smallest absolute gap between the medians (in ascending order) are 2014, 2015, and 2013, with respective differences of \$0, \$64, and \$152.
